[{"id":"VRCE-5-32-1","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":1,"english":"Hearing those words of Sītā stricken with grief, Hanumān, the foremost of the monkeys, replied in soothing words, O worshipful lady, by Rāma's decree I come as a messenger to these. He is sale and sound, O Vaidehi, and has asked of your welfare.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.757,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032001","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dd0542c2cf1f774d091258d0b28feae5366159d2e0c33ba7d0859eb2193f3802","compact":"fd600a32658d637f08ad5c1109d6cb03bb448c79ebb125569816c41ae2581b0d","nasal":"b1ac19799ef221a461b0d3321b079016d612e4db1d97513a8b976fab9dfba669"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-2","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":2,"english":"Hearing those words of Sītā stricken with grief, Hanumān, the foremost of the monkeys, replied in soothing words, O worshipful lady, by Rāma's decree I come as a messenger to these. He is sale and sound, O Vaidehi, and has asked of your welfare.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.763,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032002","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"34e3da77093a4230e1245e1d44e8d4cc3be8a8c84c1850f3c7034d9587c04444","compact":"e5bab55d1319a76b6352c40e8063ea7f00690ddb24763082e487eadb2c0c56d2","nasal":"e5bab55d1319a76b6352c40e8063ea7f00690ddb24763082e487eadb2c0c56d2"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-3","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":3,"english":"O worshipful dame, Rāma, the son of Dasaratha, the master of the Vedas, and the foremost of those conversant with Vedas has enquired of your welfare.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032003","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"46fcaf3162b9f3c50f627531765ac704c0020e1a8ddb2189e5f4f07780a039a1","compact":"e03a06dce729b9954e366621daf68bfde65464d19db28adc313af90ee6627b33","nasal":"e03a06dce729b9954e366621daf68bfde65464d19db28adc313af90ee6627b33"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-4","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":4,"english":"And the highly effulgent Lakşmaņa, the beloved follower of your husband, worn with grief, bows his reverential head to you.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.871,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032004","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"694a30fa231fe15e8376dfb869c2bd08a4342a0d4423fc73ac2c005dcd03ce4b","compact":"835a782bdd303670fa3fe3e007accbe207c3bb93ac992723510b407e0895a952","nasal":"835a782bdd303670fa3fe3e007accbe207c3bb93ac992723510b407e0895a952"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-5","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":5,"english":"Hearing of the welfare of those two lions among men, the worshipful dame, with all her frame worked up with rapture, again spake to Hanumān, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.746,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032005","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"34b6c7826e7f26c60f47d51f64b94f1a42e219c2870b56166e5d00f0525806f9","compact":"c19280fcfc405cf22a6f24f6a45c3b623f2d8de7ad8244c1f026812da1d412c9","nasal":"c1aaf29aa1ce3ce80e7af02a47e6607c70235bd37573022c53ab4980e587db53"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-6","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":6,"english":"Verily the wise saw of the people appears to me to be true that once in a hundred years true joy appears to a living man.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.471,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032006","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f601a5e092550e256e86c066fc438dff0d6893a571d6f7c1ef6acb0a4dc9beee","compact":"8c58e3af37f7833d266708dcac802d3df2d25ca8cb644630090d68cd3904a897","nasal":"7e28d44f4b718328154eb38f2f0105485a3af6b37bfbec11e059c802624e7153"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-7","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":7,"english":"Sītā, at that time, attained such a wonderful joy, as would have been acquired by her in the company (of Rama and Laksmana). And they confidingly conversed with each other.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032007","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f70f6e69dbc1f8a0979a67dcfb0151f8ea89ce0d35d443f1a457f614ffe1ec8d","compact":"3942781b047e7f54b0e93690f2d05c6c009475297507df065f594810a5a9dc8d","nasal":"3942781b047e7f54b0e93690f2d05c6c009475297507df065f594810a5a9dc8d"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-8","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":8,"english":"Hearing those words of $ītā, racked with sorrow, Hanumān, the son of the Wind-god drew a little nearer to her.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.6,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032008","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d0119df6ca29e6cdd3cff8fdc2b479a240769ebc51b593da8b697b5aa70ae1f1","compact":"ce6171e088749d558df352c25ab5aae35e73ed105d82569b4e85f8a40c61be2f","nasal":"ce6171e088749d558df352c25ab5aae35e73ed105d82569b4e85f8a40c61be2f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-9","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":9,"english":"The more Hanumān approached her the more Sītā suspected him as Rāvaņa.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032009","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0e29f97df3fd10a84d7a19c2cad290ac3c01faa2204be5eae21480ebdae38593","compact":"926c966b711afbe3bf21ff8d62b057d1dcb6568cc6df52773dc1b14c3762a272","nasal":"e073c22383467680143c53182c1efab7a35e6e468c38d96a7f3ff90662ce2560"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-10","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":10,"english":"Alas! Alas! why have I spoken to him? It is the Rāvaņa, who has appeared again in a new disguise.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032010","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9e183eca37c6904d269d141cf7cb74c523912f41e9927a2b4e664a6c757a870a","compact":"ca71b1ab279b6499f9e9622435af77509600b99d8c2c7040dff1f9b03454b529","nasal":"2b1491f43a89af6bccbe7eaca92995d85c939917ce785f10ffd84348b6e04629"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-11","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":11,"english":"And leaving off the Asoka branch, that one having a blameless person, overwhelmed with grief, sat down on the earth.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.862,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032011","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"28b130eec9643c88688407d5959004a27920675cb50ecff462953969997047d2","compact":"7baabb7f1016052aeffb1153e2bce2e09e59718e4277a48747b6f01b6d4d784e","nasal":"672dc69a760b52c83654ebf485966b90b3ab3d01aa928b3176d7d4581e5e730f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-12","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":12,"english":"In the meantime the mighty-armed monkey bowed to Janaka's daughter, but she, worked up with fear, did not cast again her looks towards him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.656,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032012","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6d00057202bf462baf22d85039856dfdfccbaa3849c3076f18d92ebfb85dc9ed","compact":"df86b4b5a4195dca81e94bc2dae8d1d0afda8391de262f0f941174fb8f27e817","nasal":"c9090f37be43769605830f9668812fcecb71171fb3b0480d122a6db902644693"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-13","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":13,"english":"Beholding him bow to her, Sítā, having a moon-like countenance, sighing, spoke to that monkey in sweet accents.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.859,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032013","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"118e50acb57e8686ed7fe6d17f73daf6e9e4b66cb290679ad410a5652594d128","compact":"2b5f0c55fd65802051301a7fb2079dcbe09e9e9011cf323541d9717cb61444c6","nasal":"73f79eaf838247805413ebad535740c29eaa3eca7dfb63838a08eecdf78446de"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-14","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":14,"english":"If you are that Ravana's self, who, changing your shape by magic art, want to distress me again— this your vile deed dose not behove you.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032014","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"db9701541bc9ffd5b214096dbe62a8f6bf67308f5901a74e5b4abb33924f1cd8","compact":"c2075a3414227400b1a100687cf1555316e253b72ac38e1a831e1e660b5162c4","nasal":"d145b6d2cb330ef2a8009f5b8d430a864decfe86e00646069d0d879495822e2a"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-15","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":15,"english":"You are surely that Rāvana, whom I saw at Janasthāna, in a mendicant's guise, renouncing his own shape.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.705,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032015","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8b0f616ed87fa6b30bd208232a0adcd766bd0068a0553f707e4f263342c0bd06","compact":"de770cac49b2ed581cbbf728e35d30d535e38c43b4d0661b0c35f47ff1431fe5","nasal":"de770cac49b2ed581cbbf728e35d30d535e38c43b4d0661b0c35f47ff1431fe5"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-16","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":16,"english":"If you are that Ravana's self, who, changing your shape by magic art, want to distress me again— this your vile deed dose not behove you. O night-ranger, O you wearing shapes at will! it dose not behove you to distress me who am poorly and famished with fasts.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.824,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-13","DUTT-5-34-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032016","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a7ab4cabad53ab511d8f57ba5767bfa93a4a114077c0447336766a0ec81216b0","compact":"bf18b3693be572615e97eecef5746a6d8832a9812cf3d14f9b1f0d53deac2523","nasal":"742e90d2e17b0bb7b337c753f96a425982e7110d2eadbab408228ad42249256a"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-17","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":17,"english":"And may good betide you if you are Rāma's messenger. I do ask you, O foremost of monkeys, something about Rāma that is pleasant to me.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032017","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7d0438be3cd1e0f1346d4ee30e1f027b0bb5082e9ce5df9e1c9c8936676fa67b","compact":"1db88e97225b68d1c906bebf1a2ae17d10c4ef236f7e2441c2b1f910c077c51a","nasal":"1db88e97225b68d1c906bebf1a2ae17d10c4ef236f7e2441c2b1f910c077c51a"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-18","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":18,"english":"O monkey, do you relate the glories of my dear Rama. You are (thus) shaking my mind O gentle one, like to water breaking down the banks of a river.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032018","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"10931c2c6e0fa0666bcb4aead4be097a1112fc14dfc1d04e6d27cc3acd474ad0","compact":"b4e68950d25c1a6106452e8c7ebeb9da3febc88e7f8b5689efe6b20ac32c5cbf","nasal":"b4e68950d25c1a6106452e8c7ebeb9da3febc88e7f8b5689efe6b20ac32c5cbf"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-19","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":19,"english":"Alas! this dream has afforded me immense pleasure since, I, who have been separated from my lord for so long a time, have beheld this monkey sent by Rāghava.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.727,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032019","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"330dcc81b7b127a00e0099da78c16656563fa807fb914d51993449242be93e3d","compact":"9540a920c260fa27bce91d217470625765935c5872a2e5efa6c5eb8f972e9c1b","nasal":"9540a920c260fa27bce91d217470625765935c5872a2e5efa6c5eb8f972e9c1b"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-20","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":20,"english":"I do not sink even if I can behold in dream the heroic Raghava. along with Laksmana-but dream even is Jealous of my well-being.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032020","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1c8669f53b96c13d655c4d4a7637f3148b9056959895e2d76070d0ebba864834","compact":"ab5d402dec37277d916dfafe3a1265f7c72e9eda8d325bc79bcad8303ac0751d","nasal":"11aee640f6405efa66a2a75afcc41039b55526f19e801a5e34c9b1decd8f431a"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-21","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":21,"english":"Nor do I regard this as dream. To behold a monkey in a dream dose not lead to prosperity whereas I have attained it. (Here prosperity means joy-Sītā attained an immense treasure of joy on beholding Rāma's messengerHanumãn.)","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032021","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"244979d352513ca4c9c4b5ab7afcf2a651e00971ebefa3b0571bdc84aca65977","compact":"da27723aed7996b45369354a90d11b3a09745c6cc356ebcd9e02a43b71449289","nasal":"da27723aed7996b45369354a90d11b3a09745c6cc356ebcd9e02a43b71449289"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-22","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":22,"english":"Or is it mental derangement? Or have I been possessed by a devil? Or have I run mad? Or is it the sultry vapour noating over sands and appearing at a distance like water?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.786,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032022","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1981d3b28ba372d5b378c56b1a6a9f1cbccfb0e23c671fe133f0b019b710f78c","compact":"a71ceeab9586a53a7428560e1fbcd9195d4dc5537ad3801ed2703aa483de8893","nasal":"a71ceeab9586a53a7428560e1fbcd9195d4dc5537ad3801ed2703aa483de8893"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-23","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":23,"english":"No, it is not madness-for derangement of mind is the sign of madness, I have not lost sense and I do distinctly perceive me and the monkey.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.77,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032023","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d253abd1d9257024d055f3aba60d4cd57e14eef82e95b4433247405e449b57c8","compact":"1e5006f959a41ad117ce85976af9a20132765f8df744724db15f7bc804356f2f","nasal":"1e5006f959a41ad117ce85976af9a20132765f8df744724db15f7bc804356f2f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-24","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":24,"english":"Thinking thus in many a way and ascertaining the strength of the monkey and the Rākṣasa) Sītā took him for the lord of the Raksasas, wearing shapes at will. And having arrived at this decision, Janaka's daughter-Sītā, ceased from talking with that monkey.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.873,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032024","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7eed6af4be75dc1ac61aefd5c909ff3c820e58cb93f9876972dc3213c6702b36","compact":"a61ed3f631fddc655b3ef13290b25d0ef8ad7f4c387de5c4f0e0fffaa94f6622","nasal":"a61ed3f631fddc655b3ef13290b25d0ef8ad7f4c387de5c4f0e0fffaa94f6622"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-25","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":25,"english":"Thinking thus in many a way and ascertaining the strength of the monkey and the Rākṣasa) Sītā took him for the lord of the Raksasas, wearing shapes at will. And having arrived at this decision, Janaka's daughter-Sītā, ceased from talking with that monkey.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032025","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9f83d77f3ab87b52b48d84f9d7fc67207ced8f30d55cf061bcd4971f3123852f","compact":"00a9f09b769947a3500ea8bb144920c3da5c8dbf1b0a95aa9587079633ab56fe","nasal":"00a9f09b769947a3500ea8bb144920c3da5c8dbf1b0a95aa9587079633ab56fe"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-26","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":26,"english":"And apprised of Sītā's thought, Hanumān, the Wind-god's son, (replied) in words sweet to her and enhancing her delight.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.693,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032026","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"598f925c7dfb6527c92b9bbbfad0728efb8d101490a51343d3c7224bc117fa2a","compact":"5058039542b217c755b02433f7fde421320b4a3f3124cba3a3918260405e117f","nasal":"6fb4fb54907faf826d6f261505a23d4f957a6859723a49a99a1e4770e9276c27"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-27","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":27,"english":"Bright as the sun that lights the sky and dear as the moon to every eye, he pleases all his subjects with bounties like to those of as Vaisravana. (Another name of Kubera, the god of gold.)","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032027","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5b792a9e8b21fc6c9cc627b6841898962ca19653305c22f01548703a3b9be95f","compact":"3e17e1b5a499da3f566354db21cf5d7633e5ec7d14cdea27a5ca0c0501d37a37","nasal":"ea0745495e5abf37fc40c54582a29332cec348502791b1074e41458502ca4b7d"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-28","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":28,"english":"He is gifted with prowess like to the greatly famed Vişnu, truthful and sweet-speeched like to Vacaspati. (The god of speech)","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032028","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"08e535fae4e080c163a924fc21c7534bd595fdf1a15d43967b93db29930f34d1","compact":"a294f04dbbb5877e059f8e43728438735b51ecc88f594f85574f96b5dc2ddbe1","nasal":"77f9d5ef5a383feb97077c589164f8c7f1b5e9da6f264306489c7f5578b5a6fa"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-29","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":29,"english":"Graceful, grateful to the eye and beautiful like to Kandarva's (Cupid or the god of love in Hindu mythology described the most beautiful of the celestials) Self, he displays his wrath in a proper quarter-the foremost of men and of a mighty car. The whole world of creation (live safe) in the shadow of the arms of that high-souled one. You shall soon see the consequences he shall reap by whom, assuming the shape of a dear, Rāghava was taken away from the hermitage and you wert stolen away from that lonely place.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.596,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-28","DUTT-5-34-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5032029","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"95fbadee0553a16bf89b7e6db11a9623ec714c96093db61bb6cdfa8ae7d1c787","compact":"e3e670539a3b6947fe85ba8f18d730aab3d0a74c5b0c52d746b9470a1d406def","nasal":"a4b196cae6d70282b4d45fe54401f3b7b1d4408b1ab326f7ecf9f9bb2b3eb05b"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-32-30","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":32,"verse":30,"english":"The whole world of creation (live safe) in the shadow of the arms of that high-souled one. You shall soon see the consequences he shall reap by whom, assuming the shape of a dear, Rāghava was taken away from the hermitage and you wert stolen away from that lonely place.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.806,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-34-29"],"status":"unrevi